WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.298
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.850
NARRATOR: Liquid refreshment
can leave its mark on the table.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.310
Coasters come between
the glass and that.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.490
But two centuries ago, coasters
were mainly placed on the rim

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.860
of the glass to keep out flies.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.010
Made of felt, they
unfortunately spread germs.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.470
So the cardboard coaster
was invented to improve

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.090
hygiene on the pub scene.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.808
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.180
Beverage coasters
protect the table,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.369
and are also a marketing tool.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.880
Raise the glass and you'll
see advertising catchphrases

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.950
and promotional graphics.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.220
In fact, some bar hoppers
consider coasters pub art

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.380
and accumulate vast collections.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.950
Production at this
facility in Germany

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.600
starts with spruce wood.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.280
In this case, it's from
the Black Forrest nearby.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.550
They spray the wood with
water to moisten the bark.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.240
Then it's into a tumbler
that tosses the wood logs.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.209
As they knock against each
other, the bark peels off.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.070
The debarked wood exits
onto a conveyor with slots

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.860
to correctly orient it for
entry into a massive grinder.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:22.000
Inside, chains press the wood
logs against a grinding stone,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.140
shredding them into chips.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.400
Water flows over the grinding
stone to cool it and carry away

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.310
the chips, which are then
mixed into a mesh that has

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.260
a porridge-like consistency.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.710
It's pure would pop.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.720
They now add leftover
product from previous beer

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.800
coaster production to the pulp.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.435
This will be used to
make the middle layer

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.235
of the beer coasters.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.500
Finally, they mix residual
paper from the manufacturer

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.020
of magazines and
envelopes with water.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.270
This creates a
lighter colored pulp.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.170
It will be used for
the printable surfaces

00:01:59.170 --> 00:01:59.970
of the beer coaster.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.479
They spray the pulp onto a
mesh that drains water from it.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.940
The fibrous wood core
goes between the layers

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:11.840
of the lighter pulp.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.990
Spickets apply a mix of wheat,
starch, and water that acts

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.295
as a glue between the layers.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.390
Off the mesh conveyers
now, the pulp board

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.900
travels between cylinders
that squeeze out the moisture.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.640
More heated cylinders dry it.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.230
Knives now cut the board
into large rectangles,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.870
sized for the coaster printing
and cutting machinery.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.860
This specialty cardboard
is known as woodpulp board.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.800
It's lightweight yet
highly absorbent.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.810
Next, it's over to the
printing department

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.510
to put the beer coaster
artwork on the wood pulp board.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.130
They use offset printing,
a process that involves

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.630
more revolving cylinders.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.340
Color cylinders pick
up ink and transfer

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.920
it to the printing cylinders.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.240
From there, the image goes to
a rubber wrapped cylinder that

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.515
then prints it onto the sheets.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.750
A print specialist scrutinizes
the print job close

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.120
up with a magnifying glass.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.880
He checks the color and
clarity of the images

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.470
and confirms that
they're correctly

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:22.640
positioned on the sheet.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.120
The borders of the images must
line up with the grit of blades

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.590
on the dye cutting machinery.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.490
If the alignment is
even slightly off,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.100
every coaster on the
sheet would be ruined,

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.470
as the dye cutter
punches out the coasters,

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.960
dozens of them in one swoop.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.020
The coasters end up
over little chutes,

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.535
and pushers give them a nudge.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.970
They fall down the chutes and
land neatly on a pallet below.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.740
The coasters stack up.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.050
And when there are a
hundred or so in each pile,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.340
they're ready for packaging.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.210
Machinery shrink
wraps the stacks.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.860
And they're ready for
the thirsty masses.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.480
This factory can produce
and package 10 million

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.120
offset printed beer
coasters every day,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.519
so there should be no
shortage at cocktail hour.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.500
They also print coasters using
a centuries-old technology

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.820
known as letterpress.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.850
Blank coasters drop
down into the machine.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.930
A rolling wheel presses each
coaster against an ink plate.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.060
It's a system that
both prints the image

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:38.830
and imbosses it
on the cardboard.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.360
It's a difference you
can both see and feel.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.750
Letterpress production is lower
volume than offset, generating

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.100
100,000 coasters per day.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.940
For coasters, the design
possibilities are endless.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.990
Just raise the glass and enjoy.

